{
  "id": "14-fabric-ontology-explained",
  "title": "Microsoft Fabric ontology: from a late shipment to a business answer",
  "script_sha256": "106d58df5d396188fb6e772cb3667cc27e9cad28e5bd8fd1cc9e23055e5807b5",
  "duration": 121.2267,
  "beats": [
    {
      "scene": "hook",
      "speaker": "Noah",
      "voice": "am_michael",
      "text": "Our shipment is late. Which customers are affected? A table called shipments is not enough to explain how the business fits together.",
      "start": 0.0,
      "end": 8.2667,
      "voice_end": 8.1067
    },
    {
      "scene": "meaning",
      "speaker": "Maya",
      "voice": "af_heart",
      "text": "That's where an ontology helps. In Microsoft Fabric IQ, it gives people and agents a shared map of business concepts, their properties, and how they connect.",
      "start": 8.2667,
      "end": 17.7707,
      "voice_end": 17.6107
    },
    {
      "scene": "question",
      "speaker": "Noah",
      "voice": "am_michael",
      "text": "So we're not just drawing a diagram?",
      "start": 17.7707,
      "end": 20.2347,
      "voice_end": 20.0747
    },
    {
      "scene": "entities",
      "speaker": "Maya",
      "voice": "af_heart",
      "text": "Right. Start with four entity types: Customer, Order, Shipment, and Warehouse. A particular shipment, like Shipment seventeen, is an instance. Its status and promised delivery time are properties.",
      "start": 20.2347,
      "end": 31.936,
      "voice_end": 31.776
    },
    {
      "scene": "relationships",
      "speaker": "Maya",
      "voice": "af_heart",
      "text": "Now name the relationships. A customer places an order. A shipment fulfills that order. A warehouse dispatches the shipment. Those labels explain what each connection means.",
      "start": 31.936,
      "end": 42.8053,
      "voice_end": 42.6453
    },
    {
      "scene": "binding-question",
      "speaker": "Noah",
      "voice": "am_michael",
      "text": "But where do the actual values come from?",
      "start": 42.8053,
      "end": 45.3333,
      "voice_end": 45.1733
    },
    {
      "scene": "bindings",
      "speaker": "Maya",
      "voice": "af_heart",
      "text": "Data bindings connect those business definitions to real sources. For example, orders can come from a lakehouse, while shipment updates come from an eventhouse. The ontology adds shared meaning; it doesn't become a second copy of the database.",
      "start": 45.3333,
      "end": 59.9573,
      "voice_end": 59.7973
    },
    {
      "scene": "demo",
      "speaker": "Maya",
      "voice": "af_heart",
      "text": "Here's a real retail ontology demo from Guy in a Cube. Watch the entities connect, then see the properties linked to their data sources. This is the interface behind the idea.",
      "start": 59.9573,
      "end": 70.784,
      "voice_end": 70.624
    },
    {
      "scene": "trace-question",
      "speaker": "Noah",
      "voice": "am_michael",
      "text": "Now let's follow our late shipment back to the customer.",
      "start": 70.784,
      "end": 74.1013,
      "voice_end": 73.9413
    },
    {
      "scene": "trace",
      "speaker": "Maya",
      "voice": "af_heart",
      "text": "Shipment seventeen fulfills Order forty two, placed by Customer one hundred one. Highlight that path, and the business question becomes specific: which customer is connected to this delayed shipment? This animation is illustrative, not a live query result.",
      "start": 74.1013,
      "end": 89.856,
      "voice_end": 89.696
    },
    {
      "scene": "verify",
      "speaker": "Maya",
      "voice": "af_heart",
      "text": "An agent can use the definitions and bindings as context, then query the permitted source. But a useful model still needs clear definitions, correct data, and answers checked against trusted results. An ontology doesn't guarantee correctness.",
      "start": 89.856,
      "end": 104.8,
      "voice_end": 104.64
    },
    {
      "scene": "recap",
      "speaker": "Noah",
      "voice": "am_michael",
      "text": "So start small: one business question, a few entities, meaningful relationships, and real source bindings.",
      "start": 104.8,
      "end": 111.7653,
      "voice_end": 111.6053
    },
    {
      "scene": "close",
      "speaker": "Maya",
      "voice": "af_heart",
      "text": "Exactly. Model the meaning, connect the data, then verify the answer. Fabric ontology is in preview, so check the current documentation as you build.",
      "start": 111.7653,
      "end": 121.2267,
      "voice_end": 121.0667
    }
  ],
